namespace __SOLUTION__.Contracts.Greetings;

/// <summary>
/// The reference slice's response. Delete it with the slice.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// <para><strong>This exists to be copied.</strong> A response record is the shape that crosses the
/// wire, so it lives here where the front end can reference it without referencing the project that
/// holds the data access. Its query stays nested in <c>Greet</c>, because nothing outside the
/// application dispatches a query and the holder is where the operation's decisions belong.</para>
///
/// <para><strong>The folders mirror.</strong> <c>Greetings/GreetResponse.cs</c> here pairs with
/// <c>Greetings/Greet.cs</c> in the application project, so finding a response is mechanical rather
/// than a search. Keep that as the solution grows -- it is the thing that makes a split operation
/// readable.</para>
///
/// <para><strong>Named flatly, not nested in a second <c>Greet</c> holder.</strong> Nesting it would
/// put two static classes called <c>Greet</c> in two namespaces, and the one file that has to name
/// both is the holder -- so the ambiguity would land exactly where the operation is read. The
/// asymmetry is deliberate.</para>
///
/// <para>An architecture test holds the arrangement: every request the application declares has its
/// response type declared in this project. Without it, the first operation written from habit nests
/// its response and the client cannot see it.</para>
/// </remarks>
/// <param name="Greeting">What to say.</param>
public sealed record GreetResponse(string Greeting);
